      <h3 class="vk-pl-row__heading">V-KOOL 70 — The Spectrally Selective Standard Chosen by the AIA and the Guggenheim</h3>
      <p class="vk-pl-row__body">V-KOOL 70 (VK70) is the flagship of the V-Kool architectural film series and the most widely specified product in the lineup. At 70% visible light transmission, it reads as nearly clear glass from inside and outside — yet it rejects 55% of total solar energy and blocks 94% of infrared radiation. The exterior reflectance is approximately 9%, which means there is no mirror-building effect and no visible tint from the street. These numbers are measured against NFRC.org official ratings, not marketing approximations.</p>
      <p class="vk-pl-row__body">The specification history of VK70 tells its own story: the American Institute of Architects headquarters in Washington D.C. evaluated multiple film brands and selected VK70. The Guggenheim Museum in New York integrated V-Kool into its skylight retrofit to preserve daylight while reducing solar gain and UV exposure on priceless artwork. Exxon specified VK70 for new stores facing high solar load. For Philadelphia Class A office towers, premium residences, and institutional buildings where aesthetics are non-negotiable, VK70 is the product that performs without showing.</p>
