      <h2 class="vk-casestudy__title">How V-Kool VK70 Solved a West-Facing Heat Problem Without Changing the Building's Look</h2>
      <p class="vk-casestudy__lead">A Rittenhouse Square commercial building chose V-Kool window film in Philadelphia — specifically VK70 — to address a chronic afternoon heat problem on its three west-facing floors — a combination of Philadelphia's hot summer sun and floor-to-ceiling glass that made those floors uncomfortable from noon onward and overworked the building's HVAC system.</p>
      <p class="vk-casestudy__body">The building management's constraint was clear: the glass facade was a design feature, and any film solution that visibly changed the appearance of the glass from the exterior was off the table. The building's ownership had turned down previous proposals for standard solar tint films precisely because the exterior reflectance or visible tint was unacceptable. V-Kool VK70 — with its 70% VLT, 9% exterior reflectance, and 55% TSER — met that constraint while delivering the heat rejection performance the building needed.</p>
      <p class="vk-casestudy__body">Window Film Philadelphia installed VK70 across three floors in three days. Post-installation, the west-facing floors went from being the most-complained-about spaces in the building to some of the most desirable — occupants reported a genuine comfort difference in afternoon hours. The building's facilities team noted a measurable reduction in cooling demand on the treated floors. The glass looks exactly as it did before the installation.</p>
      <blockquote class="vk-casestudy__quote">
        <p>&ldquo;We specified V-Kool VK70 for our entire west-facing facade at our Center City headquarters. The glass still looks completely clear from inside and outside, but the afternoon heat problem we had on three floors is completely resolved. Our HVAC team noticed the difference in cooling load within the first billing cycle.&rdquo;</p>
